A||||9||WisconsinGT||88GT Shocks/Struts||||1
Z||000000||WisconsinGT||10-23-1999||05:04 PM||||Well, it is time to get new Shocks/Struts. What are they by the way, Shocks or Struts? Also, which ones would you recommend? (Monroe?) Anyone have any idea what it costs to have a shop do this? Thanks<P>While taking off the rear wheels, I noticed that when I turned the tire it had about an inch of play. (The car was in park and it is an automatic.) Is this something to be concerned about?<P>Lets see...While driving the other day, I noticed that I was low on gas. As I proceeded to get to a gas station, I noticed that the car backfired and started missing. The funny thing about it, was that if I did not make any sharp turns, or step on the gas all I could go for anoth two or three miles. Once I fueled up everything was fine. Am I overreacting to an obvious answer. Low fuel!<P>On another note. Anyone have any idea what the little oblong window (similar to the odometer window) is on the dash above the tachometer? ||||reg||1
Z||000001||ChadMan||10-23-1999||06:08 PM||||I have KYB shocks and struts on my 88 Coupe that I got from the Fiero Store.  I've put about 35K on them and have been very pleased.<P>The slot above the tach is just there for symmetry with the other side (where the odometer is).  I think I read this in the Fiero Press Guide.||||reg||14
Z||000002||theogre||10-23-1999||11:23 PM||||KYB is also available thru most pep boys, etc. shop them first.<P>to answer the other question shocks in front struts in back.<P>you can change struts yourself. take out the 2 bolts at the bottom, then the 3 nuts on top. DO NOT remove the nut in the center on top! Take the whole assembly to the local parts store and they can swap the parts around for a few dollars. Then you put the whole thing back in, and go get an alignment done.<P>If you can borrow the spring compressor someplace then you can even do that work yourself but you have to be very carefull. that spring can kill you if it gets loose.<P>an inch of rotation? that's normal. your taking up the play in the entire drive train between the wheel and the park lock inside the tranny.<P>the back firing was just because you were so low you were losing fuel presure when the fuel ran away from the pump.<BR><p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 10-23-1999).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||88 Fiero||10-24-1999||11:07 AM||||Wisconsin...regarding the fuel part...don't wonder about it.. unfortunately our Fiero doesn't have a light that tells us if the fuel is low and it doesn't tell you how far it could run on almost empty tank...it happened to me too..unfortunately when it happend I was going to race a on old honda... that M*#@%$ F&@%*&# took off and I was way behind...but my car is surging really bad..I thought what the hell happened...I know I need gas that night..Anyway, after putting gas the car ran fine...I am in the process to see if I could put a sensor for the gas..that is a totally pisser!!!!||||reg||1
Z||000004||terryk||10-24-1999||12:46 PM||||The baffle trays in the tanks are prone to breaking. On a low tank, the pump starves.||||reg||1
Z||000005||fiero56||10-24-1999||10:54 PM||||I've always wondered about the possibility of adding a low fuel light.  The best I've come up with, is wiring it up to the up-shift light.  I believe that it was only used on the V-6 in 1985. Not really sure about the 4-cylinder.  Is it possible to wire a low fuel light on a car that didn't have it?  I sure miss that light.  My Sable had one, loved it.  I  also loved that car though.(I miss it)||||reg||1
Z||000006||GT Bastard||10-26-1999||03:16 AM||||I've heard good things about the KYB's, but you can't beat Monroe for value.  I put the better quality monroe stuff on all four corners recently(roadhandlers) and I love them.  Price-wise, I think KYB kindof rapes people for their stuff. ||||reg||1
Z||000007||Carrolles||10-26-1999||06:33 AM||||On my 85 SE v-6 4spd the extra rectangular window above the tach is the upshift light.||||reg||1
Z||000008||Bill Osmer||10-26-1999||09:15 AM||||fiero56 -<BR>I'm working on exactly what you described!! (You've stolen my thunder) - When my 86 GT' s fuel gage reads empty, it's EMPTY!. So, I've been working on replacing the upshift light with a fuel tank warning symbol (have you seen a graphic that I can copy??). The electronics will fit on a small PC board, and attach to the fuel gage signal. Minotr modifications/additions. I could go as far as generate a PC board if there is enough interest...<BR><p>[This message has been edited by Bill Osmer (edited 10-26-1999).]||||reg||4
Z||000009||deceler8||10-26-1999||10:05 AM||||How about a graphic of a man holding out his thumb for the low fuel light ?<P>I've had the same experience...almost a surging when the car is nearing the empty mark. <P>Now I don't let it get below 1/4 and don't have the problem.||||reg||1
